name,table_csv,reference,notes
hamamatsu_s1223,hamamatsu_s1223.mietab,"Hamamatsu S1223 Si PIN photodiode datasheet KPIN1050E02 (Feb 2013), datasheet table point",Si PIN photodiode QE curve (4 datasheet points 660-960 nm)
toshiba_tcd1304ap,toshiba_tcd1304ap.mietab,"Toshiba TCD1304DG/AP CCD linear image sensor datasheet (Toshiba Electronic Devices & Storage Corp., Rev.1.3, 2019-01-08), 'Typical Performance Curves' Spectral Response chart (relative response, peak normalized to 1.0 at ~550nm); absolute QE scale is an ASSUMED peak of 55% (typical of front-illuminated Si linear CCDs of this class, cf. Janesick, 'Scientific Charge-Coupled Devices', SPIE Press 2001) -- Toshiba only publishes RELATIVE response, not absolute QE","3648-element linear CCD used in the Ocean Optics/Ocean Insight USB4000 spectrometer (per the USB4000 Data Sheet, doc 211-00000-000-05-201210); curve points DIGITIZED by eye from the datasheet's relative-response plot (400-1050nm), estimated read error +-0.03 relative units; the Ocean Optics USB4000 Data Sheet explicitly notes 'Ocean Optics applies a coating to all TCD1304AP detectors, so the optical sensitivity could vary from that specified in the Toshiba [bare-chip] datasheet' -- this row models the BARE Toshiba chip, not the as-coated instrument; HIGH confidence for relative shape/peak wavelength (real datasheet), MEDIUM-LOW for the absolute 55% scale (assumed, not measured)"
sony_ilx511b,sony_ilx511b.mietab,"Sony ILX511 2048-pixel CCD linear image sensor datasheet (Sony Corp., doc E94108-TE), 'Spectral Sensitivity (typ.)' chart (relative sensitivity, peak normalized to 1.0 at ~450nm, Ta=25C); absolute QE scale is an ASSUMED peak of 55% (typical of front-illuminated Si linear CCDs of this class, cf. Janesick, 'Scientific Charge-Coupled Devices', SPIE Press 2001) -- Sony only publishes RELATIVE sensitivity, not absolute QE","2048-pixel CCD used in the Ocean Optics USB2000/USB2000+ spectrometer (per the USB2000 OEM Data Sheet); this row is named for the later ILX511B revision per the task's request, but the digitized curve is from the base ILX511 datasheet -- no separate ILX511B spectral-response datasheet was located; the two are understood to be pin/spec-compatible silicon of the same design family (Ibsen Photonics detector-comparison notes), so the shape/peak-wavelength should carry over, but this is an ASSUMPTION, not a B-suffix-specific measurement; curve points DIGITIZED by eye from the datasheet plot (400-1000nm), estimated read error +-0.03-0.05 relative units in the wavy 500-750nm region (real fine ripples not fully resolved at this point density); HIGH confidence for gross shape/peak wavelength (real base-part datasheet), MEDIUM-LOW for the absolute 55% scale (assumed) and for B-variant applicability (assumed)"
hamamatsu_s3904,hamamatsu_s3904.mietab,"Hamamatsu S3901-1024Q/S3904-2048Q NMOS linear image sensor datasheet (Hamamatsu Photonics K.K., Solid State Division, Cat. No. KMPD1049E06, Jun. 2024), 'Spectral response (typical example)' chart giving ABSOLUTE photosensitivity R in A/W (Ta=25C), plus tabulated peak sensitivity wavelength lambda_p=600nm and spectral response range (10% of peak) 200-1000nm","QE(lambda) computed HONESTLY from the datasheet's own absolute units via QE=R(A/W)*1240/lambda_nm (standard photon-energy conversion), not assumed; R(lambda) itself DIGITIZED by eye from the 0-0.3 A/W plot (200-1200nm), estimated read error +-0.01 A/W (+-10-15% relative, worse in the 200-450nm region where the plot shows small oxide-interference ripples not resolved at this point density); the resulting QE curve (peak ~49.6% at 600nm) reproduces the datasheet's two EXACT published numbers (lambda_p=600nm, and QE falling to ~10% of peak by ~1000nm) as a consistency check; HIGH confidence overall (absolute-unit datasheet, physics-based conversion, cross-checked against two exact published values)"
